// `(alloc t)` OF A BUFFER IS THE TABLE OF ITS CONTENTS AT THAT POINT, and a store
// after it must not show through (tables.md §2).
// expect: 7 7
// inputs: 0 1
// Was built -checked: the term analysis gave these elements no bound
// (bounds-2026-09-24). The IR's interval domain bounds them, and every operation
// is proven on every target (irstep4c-2026-09-26).
//
// The meaning is (alloc t) = (alloc (table (len t) (fn (i) (t i)))). Every
// backend returned t itself for a t that is not a rule — η-tab, which is true of
// an immutable table and false of a buffer — so after `(set b1 0 9)` the table
// allocated from b1 read 9, and every target answered 9 (found lowering `alloc`
// to the IR, irstep1-2026-09-25 §4). Four targets agreeing on a wrong answer is
// why a differential suite needs a hand-computed `expect`.
fn run(n) {
    (b2, v) = {
        var b := array { 0 : _ ∈ 0..1 }
        b[0] := 7
        t = alloc(b)
        b[0] := 9
        (b, if 0 < len(t) { t[0] } else { 0 })
    }
    v
}
